52
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:30,520
OK.

53
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:34,850

54
00:03:34,850 --> 00:03:40,680
I will share three moments,
I think the most important

55
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:47,310
three moments in the
development of language model.

56
00:03:47,310 --> 00:03:50,670
The first moment is
called birth moment.

57
00:03:50,670 --> 00:03:55,740
Actually, I got into
the area at this moment.

58
00:03:55,740 --> 00:04:02,310
It's very honored that I'm
among the first group of people

59
00:04:02,310 --> 00:04:06,240
who published papers on the
next year, the [INAUDIBLE],

60
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:08,680
when BERT came out.

61
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:13,860
And at that time, things is--

62
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:17,019
we don't really know what
is language modeling.

63
00:04:17,019 --> 00:04:21,645
So at that time,
nearly all the people

64
00:04:21,645 --> 00:04:28,450
are talking about how can we get
a better self-supervised method

65
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:30,420
in our machine.

66
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:32,610
At that time, a
common opinion is

67
00:04:32,610 --> 00:04:40,630
masked language model is as
good as understanding the text.

68
00:04:40,630 --> 00:04:43,530
And GPT, the
autoregressive model,

69
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:46,860
is better for text generations.

70
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:54,980
And T5 maybe can do them
both but is redundant.

71
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:56,330
And that's true.

72
00:04:56,330 --> 00:05:06,590
But nowadays, we all say
that GPT has now a nearly--

73
00:05:06,590 --> 00:05:09,590

74
00:05:09,590 --> 00:05:12,050
silver bullet or other anarchy.

75
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:16,100

76
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:21,410
Sometimes the things
changes, and we

77
00:05:21,410 --> 00:05:26,720
will back from that
time point and know

78
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:31,070
how the language
model things and how

79
00:05:31,070 --> 00:05:34,580
we got more and more knowledge
about language model.

80
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:39,560
So at that time, the--

81
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:43,670
I'm also among one
of them who want

82
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:50,120
to develop a new self-supervised
learning method for [INAUDIBLE].

83
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:54,440
We published a paper
called GLM, and we

84
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:58,970
want to unify the BERT,
the masked language

85
00:05:58,970 --> 00:06:02,090
model, and the
autoregressive model,

86
00:06:02,090 --> 00:06:10,340
and T5 in decoder-only style.

87
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:13,380
Actually, the method
is very simple.

88
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:20,270
We just select a
part of the sequence

89
00:06:20,270 --> 00:06:27,230
and only do autoregressive
modeling during this sequence.

90
00:06:27,230 --> 00:06:33,320
So if we select the mask
area as all the sequence,

91
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:34,730
it become a GPT.

92
00:06:34,730 --> 00:06:37,540
And part of them,
it becomes BERT.

93
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:43,930
So that's a method we
found very efficient.

94
00:06:43,930 --> 00:06:48,240
And because we train it, it's
like a BERT, it's about 15%

95
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:51,790
of the masked area, and they
perform better than BERT.

96
00:06:51,790 --> 00:06:53,490
We trained it as a GPT.

97
00:06:53,490 --> 00:06:56,670
It perform the same as GPT.

98
00:06:56,670 --> 00:07:00,926
It's very promising.

99
00:07:00,926 --> 00:07:05,690

100
00:07:05,690 --> 00:07:09,710
The second moment I
think is very important

101
00:07:09,710 --> 00:07:13,340
is the GPT-3 moment.

102
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:19,140
It tells us the scaling
law is very important.

103
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:23,880
So you can design
different architectures,

104
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:30,690
define different laws,
different self-supervised tasks

105
00:07:30,690 --> 00:07:36,180
and different methods to
schedule different models,

106
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:42,860
but the performance maybe
has some upper bound.

107
00:07:42,860 --> 00:07:47,340
But so if you add
more compute, you

108
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:54,420
can get a guaranteed
performance improvement.

109
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:59,880
You can predict the
result's perplexity

110
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:05,250
based on the fitted curve.

111
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:10,960
So at that time, the
language modeling

112
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:14,440
has become more and
more engineering.

113
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:18,490
If you have find
a very good point,

114
00:08:18,490 --> 00:08:20,500
you train a language model.

115
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:26,650
If you want to scale it
and your boss give you

116
00:08:26,650 --> 00:08:32,710
four times of monies, you can
buy four times the compute.

117
00:08:32,710 --> 00:08:41,200
You just assign the compute
four more parameters or training

118
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:43,480
more tokens.

119
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:47,680
This is called scaling law.

120
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:54,250
They tell you how you can
assign different potential

121
00:08:54,250 --> 00:08:55,630
of your monies.

122
00:08:55,630 --> 00:09:04,650
So at that time, the
language model don't really

123
00:09:04,650 --> 00:09:13,470
need some architectural
innovation or algorithm

124
00:09:13,470 --> 00:09:20,680
innovation, so it's become
a engineering thing.

125
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:28,210
And the third moment, I
think, which is more important

126
00:09:28,210 --> 00:09:31,060
is called ChatGPT moment.

127
00:09:31,060 --> 00:09:36,040
At that moment, it tells
us a very important fact

128
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:39,340
is task adaptation is cheap.

129
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:47,180
And what is very important is
the knowledge from pretraining.

130
00:09:47,180 --> 00:09:49,850
This is a very bitter lesson.

131
00:09:49,850 --> 00:09:55,040
So I have told you
that at that time,

132
00:09:55,040 --> 00:10:00,350
we designed different losses,
different architectures,

133
00:10:00,350 --> 00:10:06,860
but the aim of design
different losses

134
00:10:06,860 --> 00:10:09,360
is to perform different tasks.

135
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:14,990
For example, the autoregressive
model cannot fill in the blank

136
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:18,740
in the sequence, but
GLM and BERT can,

137
00:10:18,740 --> 00:10:23,280
so we use different
pretraining task.

138
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:29,520
But currently, we know that the
task adaptation is very cheap.

139
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:32,570
You just need to fine
tune your language model

140
00:10:32,570 --> 00:10:36,940
at the final period.

141
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:43,640
The only important thing
is your pretraining loss.

142
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:50,420
The last figure is
from InstructGPT.

143
00:10:50,420 --> 00:10:55,760
This is actually the paper
about ChatGPT and how can

144
00:10:55,760 --> 00:11:00,380
we align a pretrained
model to a chat model.

145
00:11:00,380 --> 00:11:07,520
It told that the alignment can
give a very huge improvement

146
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:13,700
on human preference compared to
the original pretrained language

147
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:14,630
model.

148
00:11:14,630 --> 00:11:22,750
And the right figure is actually
a recent paper in our company.

149
00:11:22,750 --> 00:11:27,410
It tells us a very
important fact.

150
00:11:27,410 --> 00:11:30,200
Maybe it's intuitive.

151
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:36,860
The fact is the performance
of downstream task

152
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:45,700
is only related to the
loss of pretraining.

153
00:11:45,700 --> 00:11:52,270
And it's not directly relevant
to the model size, which

154
00:11:52,270 --> 00:12:01,560
means if a large model which a
very high loss because of lack

155
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:02,580
of training.

156
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:07,320
And a small model, we
train more and reach

157
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:09,750
the same level of loss.

158
00:12:09,750 --> 00:12:16,590
They performed exactly the
same in the downstream tasks.

159
00:12:16,590 --> 00:12:21,960
So the so-called
emergent ability

160
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:31,550
and some other maybe ability,
strange rumors are not true.

161
00:12:31,550 --> 00:12:38,400
Actually, the ability is not
from the number of parameters

162
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:39,390
of language model.

163
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:46,720
It's actually only relevant to
the loss of your language model.

164
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:53,610
So all the language model
became a game of curve fitting.

165
00:12:53,610 --> 00:13:01,596
This is actually the
current situation

166
00:13:01,596 --> 00:13:04,320
of language model research.

167
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:11,040
So there's also some technical
details of large language model.

168
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:16,110
Even we know it's
not curve fitting,

169
00:13:16,110 --> 00:13:21,000
but there's a lot
of important things.

170
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:29,730
So back from some basics and
talk about the transformer

171
00:13:29,730 --> 00:13:31,560
architecture.

172
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:36,660
A very interesting thing is
the most important improvements

173
00:13:36,660 --> 00:13:41,910
nowadays are still from the
first author of the transformer

174
00:13:41,910 --> 00:13:47,260
paper, the Noam, and maybe
from his other papers.

175
00:13:47,260 --> 00:13:53,790
So actually, the real
innovation in the architectures

176
00:13:53,790 --> 00:13:56,370
is very small.

177
00:13:56,370 --> 00:14:03,240
I can summarize some common
adaptation on transformer

178
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:04,710
currently.

179
00:14:04,710 --> 00:14:06,490
First is decoder only.

180
00:14:06,490 --> 00:14:10,530
The original transformer is a
encoder-decoder architecture,

181
00:14:10,530 --> 00:14:20,250
so it's redundant because
the encoder and the decoder

182
00:14:20,250 --> 00:14:25,380
should learn how to
understand the task

183
00:14:25,380 --> 00:14:26,895
from different parameters.

184
00:14:26,895 --> 00:14:29,200
So it's redundant.

185
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:35,070
And currently we only care about
decoder-only architectures.

186
00:14:35,070 --> 00:14:38,280
The second one is
pre-layer norm.

187
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:40,450
In the original
transformer layer,

188
00:14:40,450 --> 00:14:45,780
the layer norm is after
the residual connection.

189
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:48,210
It's called a posterior norm.

190
00:14:48,210 --> 00:14:53,240
And currently, we usually
use pre-layer norm.

191
00:14:53,240 --> 00:14:58,130
The rotary position embedding
is something very special

192
00:14:58,130 --> 00:15:00,135
because it's not
published from a paper.

193
00:15:00,135 --> 00:15:00,635
It's not.

194
00:15:00,635 --> 00:15:06,086
It's published from
a Chinese blog.

195
00:15:06,086 --> 00:15:13,730
But currently, it has
proven very efficient.

196
00:15:13,730 --> 00:15:18,140
And the group query
attention is actually

197
00:15:18,140 --> 00:15:20,720
from another paper of Noam.

198
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:25,200
It can save the
inference memory.

199
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:28,680
And GLU warrant
is also from Noam.

200
00:15:28,680 --> 00:15:34,740
It's just a
replacement of the MLP.

201
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:41,020
And mixture-of-expert is
actually also from Noam's paper.

202
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:45,090
And you can use the same
FLOPs, small parameter

203
00:15:45,090 --> 00:15:47,940
to get better performance.

204
00:15:47,940 --> 00:15:55,410
So this is what's the
current and most of the ones

205
00:15:55,410 --> 00:15:59,640
open-source language model, the
architecture of most advanced

206
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:00,880
open-source language model.

207
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:03,700
For example, Llama.

208
00:16:03,700 --> 00:16:04,840
OK.

209
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:07,510
We know there's
architecture, but how

210
00:16:07,510 --> 00:16:14,080
to train this transformer
is also very important.

211
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:19,480
We need to prepare
a very powerful code

212
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:23,590
base to train the
large language model.

213
00:16:23,590 --> 00:16:27,170
So the first choice
is called DeepSpeed.

214
00:16:27,170 --> 00:16:31,230
It's a library from Microsoft.

215
00:16:31,230 --> 00:16:35,610
And some of the most
important optimization method

216
00:16:35,610 --> 00:16:41,570
is from a paper called
ZeRO from DeepSpeed group.

217
00:16:41,570 --> 00:16:45,320

218
00:16:45,320 --> 00:16:50,540
Several years ago, some
of us not really know

219
00:16:50,540 --> 00:16:55,850
how to train a very large model,
how to efficiently train them,

220
00:16:55,850 --> 00:17:00,000
but ZeRO give us some advices.

221
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:08,390
For example, we can find
the most memory consumption

222
00:17:08,390 --> 00:17:12,140
is actually the Adam states.

223
00:17:12,140 --> 00:17:15,380
The optimizer
states, you must keep

224
00:17:15,380 --> 00:17:20,550
it's for position as a float.

225
00:17:20,550 --> 00:17:23,760
And the master weight
is also a float.

226
00:17:23,760 --> 00:17:28,280
The parameter and gradient,
you can keep it high precision,

227
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:36,430
and you can have a fast
computation and save memories.

228
00:17:36,430 --> 00:17:40,190

229
00:17:40,190 --> 00:17:44,990
But the ZeRO1 can scatter the
master weight and optimizer

230
00:17:44,990 --> 00:17:48,530
state into all the
data parallel racks.

231
00:17:48,530 --> 00:17:55,130
So if you have more
racks, more GPU cards,

232
00:17:55,130 --> 00:18:05,810
you just use less GPU
memory for each rank.

233
00:18:05,810 --> 00:18:09,890
Another important technique is
called activation checkpointing.

234
00:18:09,890 --> 00:18:16,700
It's actually recall the
intermediate state and recompute

235
00:18:16,700 --> 00:18:18,080
going backward.

236
00:18:18,080 --> 00:18:24,080
So we don't really need to
record all the computation flow

237
00:18:24,080 --> 00:18:24,870
graph.

238
00:18:24,870 --> 00:18:30,830
We just need to record
some of the hidden states.

239
00:18:30,830 --> 00:18:36,500
It's reduce all the
activation from many layers

240
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:37,910
into one layers.

241
00:18:37,910 --> 00:18:45,210
And there's other methods to
reduce the memory consumption.

242
00:18:45,210 --> 00:18:48,170
For example, ZeRO2
CPU offload, which

243
00:18:48,170 --> 00:18:52,310
means you can offload
some GPU memory to CPU.

244
00:18:52,310 --> 00:18:59,940
And ZeRO3, also called
fully-sharded data parallel.

245
00:18:59,940 --> 00:19:05,040
You can just shard your
model into different cards.

246
00:19:05,040 --> 00:19:09,020
And when you use the parameter,
you gather this parameter

247
00:19:09,020 --> 00:19:10,460
from the other racks.

248
00:19:10,460 --> 00:19:16,520
So all of this method
is very complicated,

249
00:19:16,520 --> 00:19:22,910
but the DeepSpeed library have
already give a very clean API

250
00:19:22,910 --> 00:19:25,300
to use it.

251
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:29,480
So currently, it's not very hard
to train a very large language

252
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:32,330
model efficiently.

253
00:19:32,330 --> 00:19:36,800
And Megatron is
another framework

254
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:38,930
