549
00:43:53,080 --> 00:43:58,990
We have some introduction about
vision language understanding.

550
00:43:58,990 --> 00:44:05,170
It's more about engineering,
but it's multi-modality.

551
00:44:05,170 --> 00:44:08,320
And another half of the
vision language research

552
00:44:08,320 --> 00:44:11,020
about image
generation and is also

553
00:44:11,020 --> 00:44:13,490
very relevant to transformers.

554
00:44:13,490 --> 00:44:20,505
So I will also introduce the
rule about image generation.

555
00:44:20,505 --> 00:44:26,520

556
00:44:26,520 --> 00:44:33,890
Three or four years
ago, we already

557
00:44:33,890 --> 00:44:36,080
know the GPT is very powerful.

558
00:44:36,080 --> 00:44:43,390
So we want to autoregressively
modeling the text generation

559
00:44:43,390 --> 00:44:47,080
for using the GPT architecture.

560
00:44:47,080 --> 00:44:52,600
So this is the work of CogView.

561
00:44:52,600 --> 00:44:59,770
It's also my work at 2022, '21.

562
00:44:59,770 --> 00:45:06,970
It's a very simple
framework because we

563
00:45:06,970 --> 00:45:11,990
know that GPT can only predict
multinomial distribution,

564
00:45:11,990 --> 00:45:18,310
so we need to find
some method to train

565
00:45:18,310 --> 00:45:21,520
the image in a discrete way.

566
00:45:21,520 --> 00:45:30,380
There is a, maybe 2020, there's
a paper called iGPT from OpenAI.

567
00:45:30,380 --> 00:45:33,850
It's trained directly
on the pixel level

568
00:45:33,850 --> 00:45:35,870
for autoregressive modeling.

569
00:45:35,870 --> 00:45:46,110
But the sequence is very
long, so you cannot train very

570
00:45:46,110 --> 00:45:47,920
high-resolution images.

571
00:45:47,920 --> 00:45:52,950
So we can reinforce the
training image tokenizer.

572
00:45:52,950 --> 00:46:01,170
It's actually a VQ way
to discrete your image

573
00:46:01,170 --> 00:46:03,540
into several tokens.

574
00:46:03,540 --> 00:46:09,840
And you prepare the
sequence of a text image

575
00:46:09,840 --> 00:46:14,250
as the text first,
image later, and you

576
00:46:14,250 --> 00:46:19,830
can use GPT to train
these kind of segments.

577
00:46:19,830 --> 00:46:22,410
And finally, during
the inference,

578
00:46:22,410 --> 00:46:27,630
you first impose the text
and then predicts token

579
00:46:27,630 --> 00:46:33,690
by token in the image, and
you can generate some image.

580
00:46:33,690 --> 00:46:39,220
This is a very simple idea and
a concurrent work called Dall-E,

581
00:46:39,220 --> 00:46:42,690
and the most
powerful work called

582
00:46:42,690 --> 00:46:46,055
Parti is from the same idea.

583
00:46:46,055 --> 00:46:52,020

584
00:46:52,020 --> 00:46:57,840
We know that we generate
an image using GPT.

585
00:46:57,840 --> 00:47:03,780
So a very natural
idea is, can we

586
00:47:03,780 --> 00:47:09,600
achieve some universal modeling
for vision language tasks?

587
00:47:09,600 --> 00:47:16,570
So if we just tokenize the
image just like the text,

588
00:47:16,570 --> 00:47:23,650
we can generate image, we can
generate text from the image,

589
00:47:23,650 --> 00:47:28,220
we can generate image from
text, and only generate text.

590
00:47:28,220 --> 00:47:31,050
So this is a very neutral idea.

591
00:47:31,050 --> 00:47:42,670
And I also did this in CogVL too
maybe two years ago, and yeah.

592
00:47:42,670 --> 00:47:49,330
The other reason it's also very
simple is just in the sequence,

593
00:47:49,330 --> 00:47:56,050
you change different position
of text and image sequence.

594
00:47:56,050 --> 00:48:00,430
If first text then image
and you mask all the things

595
00:48:00,430 --> 00:48:04,960
as text-to-image generation,
you force image then text,

596
00:48:04,960 --> 00:48:06,280
it's image captioning.

597
00:48:06,280 --> 00:48:16,105
And you can also get other
formats like masked auto-encoder

598
00:48:16,105 --> 00:48:17,890
or something like that.

599
00:48:17,890 --> 00:48:25,570
But the problem is when you
compare this universal modeling

600
00:48:25,570 --> 00:48:31,000
system to diffusion or visual
language modeling with a vision

601
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:35,320
language model, you will
find the image generation

602
00:48:35,320 --> 00:48:42,010
is worse than the diffusion and
very slow compared to diffusion.

603
00:48:42,010 --> 00:48:46,870
For image understanding
it performed worse

604
00:48:46,870 --> 00:48:55,570
than vision language model,
because when you transform

605
00:48:55,570 --> 00:49:02,440
your image into discrete
tokens, lots of information

606
00:49:02,440 --> 00:49:06,590
is lost during this process.

607
00:49:06,590 --> 00:49:11,980
So the performance is worse
than the vision language model.

608
00:49:11,980 --> 00:49:18,880
So using this method, you can
achieve universal modeling,

609
00:49:18,880 --> 00:49:23,065
but you just achieve
universal modeling,

610
00:49:23,065 --> 00:49:29,990
and you cannot achieve the
best performance on any task.

611
00:49:29,990 --> 00:49:34,265

612
00:49:34,265 --> 00:49:38,230
So the diffusion
method actually wins

613
00:49:38,230 --> 00:49:42,340
the game of image generation,
and not the autoregressive.

614
00:49:42,340 --> 00:49:47,770
Although in the MLP domain
the autoregressive method

615
00:49:47,770 --> 00:49:51,010
is dominant, but in
image generation,

616
00:49:51,010 --> 00:49:53,200
the winner is diffusion.

617
00:49:53,200 --> 00:49:56,080
So what is a diffusion?

618
00:49:56,080 --> 00:50:04,600
Diffusion is a totally different
self-supervised learning method

619
00:50:04,600 --> 00:50:08,770
compared to
autoregressive methods.

620
00:50:08,770 --> 00:50:14,740
You can also think it's a
autoregressive on a Fourier

621
00:50:14,740 --> 00:50:16,280
domain or something like that.

622
00:50:16,280 --> 00:50:26,180
But actually it's the DDPM is
the original paper of diffusion

623
00:50:26,180 --> 00:50:26,680
model.

624
00:50:26,680 --> 00:50:31,510
It's still the most popular
framework of diffusion modeling.

625
00:50:31,510 --> 00:50:37,960
We can define a lot of
steps as we gradually

626
00:50:37,960 --> 00:50:43,330
add in noise to a
clean image and we

627
00:50:43,330 --> 00:50:47,500
get different
intermediate states

628
00:50:47,500 --> 00:50:53,290
and train your model to predict
the noise, the original image,

629
00:50:53,290 --> 00:51:01,870
or something like v [INAUDIBLE]
of the angle of the [INAUDIBLE]

630
00:51:01,870 --> 00:51:07,070
and are actually given the
noisy input, noisy image.

631
00:51:07,070 --> 00:51:16,540
So it's totally different,
but the most advantage

632
00:51:16,540 --> 00:51:19,090
of differential model
or autoregressive model

633
00:51:19,090 --> 00:51:36,710
is that during sampling, we
can use full utility of GPUS

634
00:51:36,710 --> 00:51:42,080
because in our regressive
model, when we decode the token,

635
00:51:42,080 --> 00:51:48,410
we actually wasted
the power of the GPU.

636
00:51:48,410 --> 00:51:52,730
The utility of GPU is very low
if the batch size is small,

637
00:51:52,730 --> 00:51:54,620
the batch size equal to 1.

638
00:51:54,620 --> 00:51:59,960
But for diffusion model,
we just input all the image

639
00:51:59,960 --> 00:52:05,480
into the model so it
can utilize the GPU

640
00:52:05,480 --> 00:52:10,260
and it can sample it much faster
than autoregressive model.

641
00:52:10,260 --> 00:52:14,790

642
00:52:14,790 --> 00:52:23,140
The Relay Diffusion
Model is our recent work.

643
00:52:23,140 --> 00:52:28,290
It solves the
problem in diffusion

644
00:52:28,290 --> 00:52:34,660
about the noise scatter
across different resolution.

645
00:52:34,660 --> 00:52:47,230
The first thing is you can see
the last image is actually three

646
00:52:47,230 --> 00:52:50,140
images with the same noise.

647
00:52:50,140 --> 00:52:55,210
The A and B are two images
with different resolution

648
00:52:55,210 --> 00:52:58,400
and with the same noise level.

649
00:52:58,400 --> 00:53:05,050
But the A is actually
more blurred for us

650
00:53:05,050 --> 00:53:06,500
during the observation.

651
00:53:06,500 --> 00:53:10,555
The problem is we add
an independent noise.

652
00:53:10,555 --> 00:53:14,530

653
00:53:14,530 --> 00:53:21,800
And actually, the
original signal, the image

654
00:53:21,800 --> 00:53:24,980
is not independent
across the space.

655
00:53:24,980 --> 00:53:37,010
So what we need to
do is if we want

656
00:53:37,010 --> 00:53:41,240
to transform a noisy
schedule from low resolution

657
00:53:41,240 --> 00:53:45,290
to high resolution, we
need to use a block noise

658
00:53:45,290 --> 00:53:54,850
to find the equivalence on
the high-resolution images.

659
00:53:54,850 --> 00:54:04,480
And finally, we can keep the SNR
in the frequency graph the same.

660
00:54:04,480 --> 00:54:14,140
So using this method, we can
disentangle the noisy schedule

661
00:54:14,140 --> 00:54:19,660
and the actually network
we use for diffusion.

662
00:54:19,660 --> 00:54:23,230
Using noisy schedule, we don't
care about the resolution.

663
00:54:23,230 --> 00:54:30,250
We just use a block noise when
we want to continue diffusion

664
00:54:30,250 --> 00:54:31,540
on high resolution.

665
00:54:31,540 --> 00:54:37,930
So the speed can
improve because we

666
00:54:37,930 --> 00:54:44,260
don't need to generate the
image from the high resolution,

667
00:54:44,260 --> 00:54:48,760
from the condition on
the low-resolution image,

668
00:54:48,760 --> 00:54:49,800
high resolution fits.

669
00:54:49,800 --> 00:54:52,985

670
00:54:52,985 --> 00:54:56,630
And we also scale up the
Relay Diffusion to CogView3

671
00:54:56,630 --> 00:55:02,470
after the paper.

672
00:55:02,470 --> 00:55:06,210
CogView3 is actually a
large diffusion model.

673
00:55:06,210 --> 00:55:10,850
And after distillation,
it could be very fast

674
00:55:10,850 --> 00:55:17,710
because of the effectiveness
of the Relay Diffusion.

675
00:55:17,710 --> 00:55:20,265

676
00:55:20,265 --> 00:55:27,070
Finally, we get to something
relevant to our topic,

677
00:55:27,070 --> 00:55:28,480
transformer.

678
00:55:28,480 --> 00:55:35,800
And actually, the previous
works about diffusion is Unet,

679
00:55:35,800 --> 00:55:42,640
and using transformer is
not trivial in diffusion.

680
00:55:42,640 --> 00:55:53,160
The first work I think maybe is
solid enough is DiT from Meta.

681
00:55:53,160 --> 00:55:56,920
The author of this paper is
also the author with ours.

682
00:55:56,920 --> 00:56:03,700
So the most difference between
the original transformer

683
00:56:03,700 --> 00:56:11,010
and this DiT is
the ada layer norm.

684
00:56:11,010 --> 00:56:16,440
Ada layer norm is
predict a scale and bias

685
00:56:16,440 --> 00:56:19,860
for different layer norm.

686
00:56:19,860 --> 00:56:23,130
And scale shifts for different
layer norm conditioning

687
00:56:23,130 --> 00:56:24,780
on the timestamp.

688
00:56:24,780 --> 00:56:29,850
It's actually needs a very
huge amount parameters,

689
00:56:29,850 --> 00:56:39,780
as the systems of hidden size
nearly equal to QKV per layer,

690
00:56:39,780 --> 00:56:44,130
but the input is only 1 int.

691
00:56:44,130 --> 00:56:49,860
1 int is actually very strange
because the input is only 1 int,

692
00:56:49,860 --> 00:56:54,700
and you need millions of
parameters to transform it.

693
00:56:54,700 --> 00:57:02,235
So there's some method can
reduce this thing in practice.

694
00:57:02,235 --> 00:57:05,740

695
00:57:05,740 --> 00:57:09,760
The Stable Diffusion
3 released recently.

696
00:57:09,760 --> 00:57:14,230
It use another
architectures called MM-DiT.

697
00:57:14,230 --> 00:57:16,510
The Stable Diffusion
3 first used

698
00:57:16,510 --> 00:57:21,400
our released CogVLM to recaption
all the model-- all the images

699
00:57:21,400 --> 00:57:23,950
and train a latent
diffusion model using

700
00:57:23,950 --> 00:57:25,400
this new architecture.

701
00:57:25,400 --> 00:57:28,090
The new architecture seem
like very complicated,

702
00:57:28,090 --> 00:57:36,920
but the most important thing is
they use vision and text experts

703
00:57:36,920 --> 00:57:42,500
like CogVLM instead of cross
attention to T5 features

704
00:57:42,500 --> 00:57:45,270
like the previous ones.

705
00:57:45,270 --> 00:57:50,340
So finally, we will talk
